{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791140605,"nonce":"loTsgD3NyM1gFxZUS/I0hJJpY8E=","message":"Implemented. Both halves were already supported by the stack: rustybuzz's\nFace derefs to ttf-parser, which has set_variation(Tag, f32), and swash's\nscaler builder takes .variations(). Blinc passed no weight into either,\nso the axis was never moved.\n\nFontFace gains a variation_weight, stamped by the registry at both\nresolution paths, the named-family one and the generic one. Both already\ncache per requested weight, so each weight gets its own face carrying its\nown axis value. set_variation_weight is a no-op on a static face, where\nthe registry's id lookup already chose the file whose own weight was\nclosest, so nothing about static families changes.\n\nThe axis is applied in BOTH shaping and rasterizing. Shaping matters as\nmuch as rasterizing here: advances differ per weight, so setting it only\nat raster time would draw heavy glyphs at regular spacing.\n\nVerified against the file this exists for. SFNS.ttf loaded directly:\n\"Handgloves\" shapes to 9822 font units at weight 200 and 11661 at 900.\nWith the axis call removed, both come back as exactly 10183, the default\ninstance, which is the bug this fixes.\n\nThe registry-level tests skip on a stock macOS, because System,\nSansSerif, Serif and Monospace all resolve to static families there.\nThey become live once GenericFont::System points at SF Pro, which is\ne974c36. The SFNS test covers the mechanism in the meantime.\n\nPrerequisite for e974c36 as recorded. Note the other prerequisite is\n2506cd7, line-height semantics: SF Pro's natural line height is about\n1.19 to 1.21em against Helvetica's exact 1.0em, so adopting it under\nBlinc's current semantics would make every line roughly a fifth taller.","files":null}]}